\frametitle{Example: a convolution computation}
\begin{columns}[T]
\begin{column}{0.48\textwidth}
\textbf{Remora AST, schematic}
\begin{lstlisting}[basicstyle=\ttfamily\small]
Call: conv-bn-mish
  input: image
  parameters: p
  definition:
    mish
      add bias
        reshape
          matrix multiply
            im2col(image)
            folded weights
\end{lstlisting}
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.48\textwidth}
\textbf{Generated Python}
\begin{lstlisting}[basicstyle=\ttfamily\small]
ops.conv(image, p,
  stride=1, pad=1,
  activation='mish',
  shape=[3,608,32,608,3])
\end{lstlisting}
\vspace{1mm}
\textbf{PyTorch runtime, schematic}
\begin{lstlisting}[basicstyle=\ttfamily\small]
w, b = fold_batch_norm(p)
y = F.conv2d(x, w, b,
             padding=1)
y = mish(y)
\end{lstlisting}
\end{column}
\end{columns}
\vspace{3mm}
One supported computation becomes a convolution operation plus activation. tinygrad uses the same generated call with its own runtime.
\note{100 seconds. Read the left as an abstract tree, not verbatim parser JSON. The actual AST has App, IApp, variable and binding nodes. The checked conv-bn-mish definition combines convolution with folded batch normalization and Mish. Its convolution implementation contains im2col, matrix multiplication and reshaping. The compiler recognizes the supported library call; it does not rediscover arbitrary convolution from this illustrative tree. The right generated call uses the real ops.conv signature, with named arguments for readability. The runtime sketch omits batch-dimension handling, shape validation and caching. One ops.conv is not necessarily one CUDA kernel. Sources: yolov4-parse.json, generated_yolov4.py and runtime.py.}
